                <div type="section" feature="local">
                    <head>LOCAL AND GENERAL.</head>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Helouan Dogs.</head>
                        <p>The pariah and ownerless dogs of Helouan are to be poisoned by the police
                            this evening and to-morrow morning.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>The Egyptian University.</head>
                        <p>The total amount collected up to date for the foundation of a national
                            Egyptian University amounts to L.E. 26,504.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Walker &amp; Meimarachi, Limited.</head>
                        <p>An official cablegram, from London announces that the Board of Directors
                            of Walker Meimarachi, Ltd, has elected Mr. James Greenlees a Director of
                            the Company.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Oysters.</head>
                        <p>The ordinance in regard to the fishing for and sale of oysters and other
                            shellfish at Alexandria has been changed and the prohibition will
                            henceforth not include "fruits de mer</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>New Alhambra Theatre.</head>
                        <p>For this evening, and to-morrow's matinée, the "Geisha" is billed at the
                            new Alhambra Theatre, and to-morrow evening "Boccacio" will be staged.
                            The "Orchid" is advertised as about to be produced.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Ras El Tin Palace.</head>
                        <p>According to a native paper the Maieh Sanieh has decided to sell by
                            public auction a considerable quantity of the old furniture of Ras el
                            Tin Palace, and has fixed the 25th instant for holding the auction.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Plague in Alexandria.</head>
                        <p>To-day's bubonic plague report for Alexandria reports one new admission
                            to hospital, the patient being an Armenian living in the Menshieh kism.
                            There are in all 12 cases under treatment in this town, of whom 8 are
                            Europeans.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Coal Imports.</head>
                        <p>Between January 1 and July 18th 704,222 tons of coal were imported into
                            Egypt, of which 378,169 tons were Welsh, 102,460 New caste, 118,127
                            Yorkshires, 57,790 Scotch, and 47,676 other qualities. During the
                            corresponding period of last year the coal imports totalled 644,053.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Hydrophobia at Alexandria.</head>
                        <p>A very important notice from the Alexandria Municipality appears in to
                            day's issue in reference to the dangers of hydrophobia. The case
                            mentioned in the notice of a mad dog at San Stefano is of special
                            interest and it is to be hoped that the Alexandria police will insist on
                            the muzzling rule being enforced.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Telegraph School.</head>
                        <p>The new school erected at Boulac by the Telegraph Administration for the
                            training of telegraphists has cost L.E. 2500. It is thought that it will
                            be necessary to enlarge this school in the near future as a greater
                            demand for telegraphists is expected in consequence of the construction
                            of new lines in the Sudan. </p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Ports and Lighthouses Administration.</head>
                        <p>The Ministry of Finance has decided to charge Dickson Bey, Chief of the
                            Port and Lighthouse district of Port-Said, with the financial affairs of
                            that Administration during the absence on leave of the Director General,
                            Vice Admiral Blomfield. The administrative and technical duties of
                            Vice-Admiral Blomfield will be undertaken by Captain Gedge, Deputy
                            Director General.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Health of Cairo,</head>
                        <p>The Egyptian Government has addressed a note to the Povers pointing out
                            the necessity of raising the tax on completed buildings to defray the
                            expenses of the projeet for the drainage of Cairo as a sanitary measure.
                            The cost of the same is estimated at LE. 1,400,000 and the government
                            propose to fix the tax at 10% of the rent, the proposed increase being
                            2%. </p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Economical Transforrnations.</head>
                        <p>The commercial transactions as well as the international social relations
                            have, in rendering more frequent and more continued the intercourse
                            between people speaking different languages, given a considerable
                            importance to the study of living languages. This is one of the reasons
                            for the success of the Berlitz schools. 1 Sharia Kamel, Cairo, 12 rue
                            Rosette, Alexandria and at the Carlton Hotel, Ramleh. [Advt.]</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Irrigation Pumps.</head>
                        <p>The Ministry of Public Works has charged Mohamed Anis Pasha, Chief of the
                            Technical Department at that Ministry, to draw up a series of
                            regulations for irrigation pumps. These regulations are intended to be
                            promulgated by the end of next year. It may be remembered that one of
                            the votes passed by the last general assembly was to request the
                            government to elaborate such regulations.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div type="item">
                        <head>Esbekieh Garden Theatre.</head>
                        <p>It appears that Mr. Gardet's troupe, which has been giving such excellent
                            entertainments during the past few weeks, in the Esbekieh Garden theatre
                            in Cairo, have been victimised by the general financial depression and,
                            although they have arrived at the end of their stay in this country
                            covered with success from a theatrical point of view, their profit has
                            been but small. A special performance of "Un fil a la patte" is to be
                            given on Wednesday evening next for the benefit of the troupe and we
                            sincerely trust that the attendance thereat will be satisfactory. The
                            French Consul has promised to be present, as have also several notables
                            of the French colony. M. Galenzi has obtained the next lease of this
                            theatre and will produce many new plays after the departure of the
                            present troupe. M. Galenzi intends to do all in his power to make the
                            Garden theatre attractive and will reduce the price of admission as a
                            preliminary step in that direction.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
